# ── Venice ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VENICE_API_KEY is required. Every model call goes through the server routes
# under src/app/api, so the key never reaches the browser (no NEXT_PUBLIC_
# prefix). Without it the UI loads and every API route answers 503 saying the
# harness isn't configured. Set a spending limit on the key in Venice as well.
#
# VENICE_API_KEY=

# ── Models ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HARNESS_MODELS is the comma-separated allow-list the model picker offers; the
# browser can never request a model outside it. Names, prices and capabilities
# are read from Venice's /models endpoint, so only the ids go here.
# HARNESS_DEFAULT_MODEL must be one of them (otherwise the first is used).
# HARNESS_UTILITY_MODEL writes chat titles and compaction summaries; pick a
# cheap one.
#
# HARNESS_MODELS=deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017-uncensored,glm-5-3-flash-uncensored
# HARNESS_DEFAULT_MODEL=deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017-uncensored
# HARNESS_UTILITY_MODEL=glm-5-3-flash-uncensored

# ── Access ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# There are no accounts yet. With HARNESS_PASSCODE set, the app shows an unlock
# screen and every API call needs the signed, HttpOnly cookie that a correct
# passcode earns (30 days). HARNESS_SESSION_SECRET signs that cookie: any long
# random string (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`); changing it signs everyone out.
#
# In production the API refuses to run with no passcode, because an open URL
# lets anyone spend your Venice credit. HARNESS_PUBLIC=1 overrides that on
# purpose. Locally (npm run dev) no passcode is needed.
#
# HARNESS_PASSCODE=
# HARNESS_SESSION_SECRET=
# HARNESS_PUBLIC=1

# ── Spend controls ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HARNESS_DAILY_CALL_BUDGET caps upstream model calls per UTC day for the whole
# process (default 2000). A chat turn costs one call per agent step; titles and
# compaction cost one each. When it is spent the API answers 503 until the next
# day. Like the rate limiter (20 chats/minute per caller), it is per process.
#
# HARNESS_DAILY_CALL_BUDGET=2000

# ── Compaction ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# When a chat's history passes this many estimated tokens (characters / 4), the
# older turns are summarised by the utility model and the summary is sent in
# their place; the last 6 messages always go verbatim. Lower it to test.
#
# COMPACT_AT_TOKENS=150000

# ── Tools (MCP) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Remote MCP servers (Streamable HTTP) whose tools the model may call, as a JSON
# array. Headers stay on the server, so put a server's auth token here. Only
# https:// URLs are accepted, plus http://localhost for local servers. Each chat
# chooses which configured servers to use in its settings.
#
# MCP_SERVERS=[{"name":"docs","url":"https://example.com/mcp","headers":{"Authorization":"Bearer ..."}}]

# ── Who is calling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Callers are rate limited by IP. On Vercel the IP comes from
# x-vercel-forwarded-for, which the platform sets and clients cannot forge;
# that header is IGNORED anywhere else. Behind another reverse proxy, set
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1 to use x-forwarded-for / x-real-ip — but ONLY if that
# proxy overwrites the header rather than appending to a client-supplied one.
# In production with neither, the API answers 503. Locally a fixed key is used.
#
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1

# ── Browser origins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Cross-site browser requests are refused: Origin must match the Host. List
# extra allowed origins here, comma-separated.
#
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://other.example
